.TH NOISSHD 1 "2026-11-07" "noissh 0.6.4" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
noisshd \- resilient remote shell over the Noise Protocol Framework (server)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B noisshd
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR ADDR ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-authorized\-keys
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-command
.IR "CMD ..." ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B noisshd \-\-one\-shot \-\-authorize
.I B64PUB
.RB [ \-\-bind
.IR ADDR ]
.RB [ \-\-command
.IR "CMD ..." ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B noisshd
is the noissh server daemon. It accepts QUIC connections over UDP secured by the
Noise XX handshake, authenticates the client's static key against
.IR authorized_keys ,
allocates a PTY, and runs the user's login shell. Connections follow a client
that changes address (QUIC connection migration); a shell whose connection is
lost keeps running, detached, for 15 minutes, and the client's next connection
takes it over.
.SH MODES
.SS Standalone
A persistent daemon using a persistent static key and
.I authorized_keys
file. This is the default mode.
.SS One\-shot
Used by the
.B noissh --ssh
bootstrap. Generates an ephemeral key, trusts only the single client key given
with
.BR \-\-authorize ,
binds the UDP port given with
.B \-\-bind
(or an ephemeral one if that port is taken), prints a line of the form
.RS
.B NOISSH CONNECT <port> <base64 server pubkey> quic
.RE
on standard output, detaches from the controlling terminal, and serves until
its session has ended. It exits if no client connects within 60 seconds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDR"
Bind address for standalone mode (default 0.0.0.0:51820).
.TP
.BI \-\-key " PATH"
Static private key file (default <config>/noisshd_key); generated on first use,
with the public key written to the same path plus
.IR .pub .
.TP
.BI \-\-authorized\-keys " PATH"
Authorized client keys (default <config>/authorized_keys). Read once at
startup: restart the daemon after adding a key.
.TP
.BI \-\-command " CMD ..."
Run this command instead of the login shell (the remaining arguments form the
command). Useful for restricted or scripted sessions.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " NAME"
Standalone mode only: drop each session to user
.I NAME
(uid/gid) before exec. Requires running
.B noisshd
as root. File transfer, agent forwarding, and remote\-command exec are refused in
this mode, because the daemon process itself does not drop privileges; prefer the
SSH bootstrap (or run the daemon as the target user) for full multi\-user use.
.TP
.B \-\-one\-shot
Ephemeral key; serve a single session, then exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-authorize " B64PUB"
In one\-shot mode, the single base64 client public key to trust.
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ADDR"
Bind address in one\-shot mode (default 0.0.0.0:0, an ephemeral port). The
.B noissh
client passes the port it wants (51820 by default); if it is taken, the
server falls back to an ephemeral port and reports it in the connect line.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log session lifecycle (each session established and ended, with the current
active session count) and fatal socket errors.
.SH SECURITY
Client and server authenticate each other inside the handshake: an
unauthorized client key fails the handshake, before any session, stream or
PTY exists. Shells run as the user
.B noisshd
runs as (no root needed). With
.B \-\-user
(as root), each shell drops to that user's uid and gid before exec, without
supplementary groups; file transfer, agent forwarding and remote commands are
then refused, since the daemon itself keeps its privileges. For multi\-user
hosts prefer the SSH bootstrap, or run one daemon per user.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOISSH_CC
Congestion control for the data this side sends: BBR by default (keeps
throughput up on lossy Wi\-Fi and mobile paths);
.B cubic
selects Cubic.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <config>/noisshd_key
Server static private key (mode 0600).
.TP
.I <config>/noisshd_key.pub
Server public key.
.TP
.I <config>/authorized_keys
Allowed client public keys, one
.I noissh-x25519 <base64>
per line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR noissh (1),
.BR sshd (8),
.BR pam (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Gerardo Di Giacomo.
